{
  "dom_id": "step-q95758",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "It is known that $43%$ of the users of a newspaper's website purchase a digital subscription. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that in a random sample of $200$ users, more than $80$ but less than $105$ of them purchase a digital subscription. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1922\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1894\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1867\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2206\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2177\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2148\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2483\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2451\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th width=\"25%\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$z$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,2.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9941\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9943\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9945\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,2.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9956\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9957\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9959\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$\\,2.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9967\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9968\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9969\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n\n</tbody></table>",
  "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.43.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.43 \\\\  & =86 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.43) \\\\  & =114 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(200)(0.43)=86\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(200)(0.43)(1-0.43)=49.02.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(86,49.02).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(80<X<105).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)=P(81\\le X\\le 104)≈P(80.5\\le Y\\le 104.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(80.5\\le Y<104.5) & =P(\\frac{80.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{104.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.79\\le Z\\le 2.64) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z<-0.79) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z\\le -0.79) \\\\  & =Φ(2.64)-Φ(-0.79) \\\\  & =0.9959-0.2148 \\\\  & =0.7811\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)≈0.781.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of\n$0.7798.$",
